#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# log

#% text
# `log(x)` gives the principal natural logarithm of `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Exact values: `log(1)` is 0, `log(e)` is 1, `log(e^q)` and `log(exp(q))` are `q` for a rational `q`, and `log(-1)` is `im*pi`.

#% item
# Other exact arguments stay unevaluated: `log(8)` is kept as it is. Use `n` for a numerical value.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values. A negative real argument gives a complex value with imaginary part `pi`.

#% item
# The branch cut runs along the negative real axis; the imaginary part of the result lies in (-`pi`, `pi`].

#% item
# `log` is listable: it applies to each element of a list.

#% item
# For a logarithm to another base, use `log_base(b, x)` or `log(b, x)`, which is the same.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
log(1)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
log(e^3)
#% output
#= 3

#% text
# A machine number:

#% cell
log(2.0)
#% output
#= 0.6931471805599453

#% text
# The logarithm to base 10 and to base 2:

#% cell
log(10.0, 5.0)
#% output
#= 0.6989700043360186

#% cell
log(2, 8)
#% output
#= 3

#% text
# The logarithm of -1:

#% cell
log(-1)
#% output
#= im*pi

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A ball, and 30 digits from an exact argument:

#% cell
log(2~30)
#% output
#= 0.693147180559945309417232121458

#% cell
n(log(2), 30)
#% output
#= 0.6931471805599453094172321214581766

#% text
# A negative machine number gives a complex value:

#% cell
log(-2.0)
#% output
#= 0.6931471805599453 + 3.141592653589793im

#% text
# A complex argument:

#% cell
n(log(1 + im))
#% output
#= 0.3465735902799727 + 0.7853981633974483im

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
log([1, e, e^2])
#% output
#= [0, 1, 2]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The logarithm undoes the exponential for a real exact argument:

#% cell
log(exp(2))
#% output
#= 2

#% text
# The exponential undoes the logarithm for any argument:

#% cell
exp(log(x))
#% output
#= x

#% text
# The derivative:

#% cell
d(log(x), x)
#% output
#= x^-1

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# `log(exp(x))` is not `x` in general, because the imaginary part of `x` may lie outside the principal range:

#% cell
log(exp(x))
#% output
#= log(exp(x))

#% text
# Products are not split into sums of logarithms:

#% cell
log(x*y)
#% output
#= log(x*y)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `exp`, `log_base`, `log_gamma`, `sqrt`, `n`
